1. First, make sure you have the `tocloft` package installed. If you haven't already, add the following line to your preamble:

```latex
\usepackage{tocloft}
```

2. Use the `tocloft` package's `newtotoc` command to define the style of your TOC. Here's an example:

```latex
\newcftitleformat{section}{\textbf}{\numberline}{1em}{\S \thecontentslabel.}{}

\newcftitleformat{subsection}{\textbf}{\numberline}{1.5em}{\thesubcontentslabel.}{}
```

3. Use the `tableofcontents` command to generate the TOC at the beginning of your document:

```latex
\begin{document}
\tableofcontents
```

4. Add the `\section`, `\subsection`, and other commands to create the sections and subsections you want to include in your TOC.

```latex
\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Background}
\subsection{Motivation}

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Data Collection}
\subsection{Data Analysis}

\section{Results}
\subsection{Descriptive Statistics}
\subsection{Hypothesis Testing}

\section{Discussion}
\subsection{Implications}
\subsection{Limitations}
```

5. The TOC will automatically generate based on the section and subsection commands you used in your document. You can adjust the formatting of your TOC using the `tocloft` package's various options. For example, to change the font size of the TOC, you can use the `\renewcommand{\cfttocfont}{\footnotesize}` command.

That's it! Your table of contents will now automatically generate based on the sections and subsections you've defined in your document. Here's an example of what the resulting TOC might look like:

```
Contents
-------- 
I. Introduction
    A. Background
    B. Motivation

II. Methods
    A. Data Collection
    B. Data Analysis

III. Results
    A. Descriptive Statistics
    B. Hypothesis Testing

